<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    ic_add — plus-sign ("create new") icon
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Referenced by:
      - fragment_chats.xml (fab_new_chat, via app:srcCompat)
      - future: ChatActivity attach button, GroupCreateActivity
        "add member" affordance. All three use this same file
        with app:tint applied at the call site. Do not duplicate.

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. MONOCHROME TEMPLATE.
       Single fill color (#FFFFFFFF), tinted at the consumer
       level (FAB's app:tint, ImageView's app:tint, ...). The
       fill is a template — the actual rendered color is
       determined by the tint. Do NOT change the fill to a
       brand color: it would be overridden by the tint, but
       it would mislead future maintainers into believing
       this file "is" a colored icon.

    2. 24dp VIEWPORT.
       Matches Material Design's standard 24dp icon grid.
       The FAB renders at 24dp by default (fabSize=normal).
       Using a smaller viewport (14dp, 18dp) would either
       stretch or produce a visually smaller-than-expected
       glyph inside the FAB. Using a larger viewport (32dp)
       would force the FAB to scale it down, at which point
       the stroke would no longer be pixel-aligned to the
       FAB's standard grid.

    3. STANDARD MATERIAL GLYPH.
       This is the canonical Material Design plus icon —
       two perpendicular 2-unit-wide strokes intersecting at
       the center of a 24-unit viewport. It is NOT a custom
       design. Users expect the FAB "+" to be exactly this
       glyph; any deviation (thicker, thinner, different
       length, different centering) reads as a rendering bug
       rather than as a design choice.

    4. STROKE THICKNESS OF 2 UNITS.
       Google's own Material Icons use 2-unit strokes at a
       24dp viewport. This produces a visual weight that
       matches the FAB's visual weight precisely. Thinner
       (1 unit) makes the plus look fragile and gets lost
       against the FAB's colored background. Thicker (3-4
       units) makes it look heavy-handed. The 2-unit stroke
       is the standard and should not be tuned.

    5. HORIZONTAL + VERTICAL STROKES, NOT A SINGLE PATH
       WITH FILL-TYPE HACKS.
       Two simple <path> elements (one horizontal bar, one
       vertical bar) produce the correct cross. Attempting
       to combine them into a single evenOdd path to "save
       a path" is a false economy — the resulting anti-
       aliasing at the intersection has a sub-pixel seam on
       some GPU drivers that reads as a faint cross-shaped
       glitch. Two overlapping rectangles painted in the
       same color is the standard Material construction and
       has no such artifact.

    6. NO androidx.AutoMirrored.
       A plus sign is not directional. Mirroring under RTL
       produces no visible change. Do not add auto-mirroring
       to this file.
    ============================================================
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">

    <!--
        Horizontal bar of the plus.
        Position: y = 11 to 13 (2 units thick), x = 5 to 19
        (14 units wide). Centered vertically (viewport center
        is y=12, bar center is y=12). Extends 5 units from
        each horizontal viewport edge, which is Google's
        standard "safe zone" for the plus icon inside a FAB.

        Path: a 14×2 rectangle. Corners are intentionally
        square, not rounded — the Material "+" glyph has
        crisp corners, not pill-rounded ends, because the
        rounded look reads as a "pill button" not a "plus."
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M5,11 L19,11 L19,13 L5,13 Z" />

    <!--
        Vertical bar of the plus.
        Position: x = 11 to 13 (2 units thick), y = 5 to 19
        (14 units tall). Mirror of the horizontal bar.

        The two rectangles overlap in the 2×2 square from
        (11,11) to (13,13). Under the default nonZero fill
        rule, both bars paint the same color, and the
        intersection double-paints to the same color — no
        seam, no artifact. This is exactly why the two-path
        approach is preferred over the single-evenOdd-path
        approach mentioned in the file header.

        Path: a 2×14 rectangle. Same corner treatment as
        the horizontal bar (square corners).
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M11,5 L13,5 L13,19 L11,19 Z" />

</vector>
